WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.024
Go provides strong foundations,

2
00:00:02.104 --> 00:00:04.880
but secure applications require
disciplined engineering.

3
00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:07.464
Treat runtime data as untrusted until it
is validated.

4
00:00:07.544 --> 00:00:09.449
Keep HTTP handlers small and reviewable.

5
00:00:09.529 --> 00:00:12.522
Convert boundary data into internal types
before sensitive use.

6
00:00:12.602 --> 00:00:15.977
Enforce authorization on the server and
near the action being performed.

7
00:00:16.057 --> 00:00:19.105
Use context, timeouts, cancellation, and
cleanup consistently.

8
00:00:19.185 --> 00:00:21.635
Handle errors without leaking sensitive
internals.

9
00:00:21.715 --> 00:00:23.239
Redact logs and protect secrets.

10
00:00:23.319 --> 00:00:25.278
Manage files, serialization, databases,

11
00:00:25.358 --> 00:00:28.188
and downstream service calls as
trust-boundary operations.

12
00:00:28.268 --> 00:00:31.915
Shared state, goroutines, and background
work should have clear ownership and

13
00:00:31.995 --> 00:00:33.574
predictable lifecycle behavior.

14
00:00:33.654 --> 00:00:36.049
Govern dependencies, builds, tests, and
releases.

15
00:00:36.129 --> 00:00:37.108
Review new packages.

16
00:00:37.188 --> 00:00:38.821
Track stale or vulnerable modules.

17
00:00:38.901 --> 00:00:42.222
Use CI checks, static analysis,
integration tests, and negative tests.

18
00:00:42.302 --> 00:00:45.785
Keep production configuration intentional
and document release changes.

19
00:00:45.865 --> 00:00:48.695
A secure Go application is not only one
that compiles cleanly.

20
00:00:48.775 --> 00:00:50.953
It is one whose trust decisions, failure
modes,

21
00:00:51.033 --> 00:00:54.244
and operational behavior are explicit,
maintainable, and testable.

22
00:00:54.324 --> 00:00:55.467
As you apply these ideas,

23
00:00:55.547 --> 00:00:58.541
look for places where the code already has
a natural review point.

24
00:00:58.621 --> 00:01:01.070
A handler can show how a request becomes
trusted data.

25
00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:03.763
A service function can show the
authorization decision.

26
00:01:03.843 --> 00:01:06.020
A repository can show how data is stored
safely.

27
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:08.985
A worker can show its timeout, cleanup,
and shutdown behavior.

28
00:01:09.065 --> 00:01:12.004
A release checklist can show how
dependencies, configuration,

29
00:01:12.084 --> 00:01:13.172
and tests were handled.

30
00:01:13.252 --> 00:01:17.063
Secure Go application development is
strongest when these controls are ordinary,

31
00:01:17.143 --> 00:01:18.340
visible, and repeatable.
